                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "In the battle arena, you control a bot and with your mechanically powered hook, you must defeat enemies either by simply destroying them or by outperforming them in a variety of different game modes. There is a brief single-player campaign (which takes about 20 minutes typically), a series of single-player challenges, a local-only multiplayer mode, a local-only party mode, and a bunch of unlockables (such as skins, characters, and alternate multiplayer modes).\n\nIn all these different modes, your character controls the same with your hook being a source of quick mobility and a lethal weapon. This is what I love about Hooktbots as this one-button allows for several interesting actions. For example, you can hold the button to latch onto a ring and swing back and forth or release it to launch in the direction that you are connected to. In addition, when latching and launching throughout the stage, almost in a spider man-like fashion, you will want to pierce your opponents. This will cause them to lose a piece of their robotic bodies, which has to be one of the coolest ways to showcase a loss of health - rather than just a simple life bar.  They will then have a chance to retrieve this body part to repair themselves or if you are quick enough you can also destroy it with your projectile arm leaving them permanently damaged. On the third lost part, robots can no longer function and burst into flames.\n\nAll these interesting split-second actions create core gameplay that is so fast-paced and tightly controlled that it is a ton of fun to play no matter what mode you choose. The one-button nature of the hooking mechanics also makes the controls easy to learn for new players (which is perfect for a party game), but still provided a lot of depth as there are more nuance movement methods to master. On top of this, there is dashing, double jumping, a handful of items, and different specials for each unique robot.\n\nIn addition, many of the levels have an upbeat energetic soundtrack to match the intensity of battles.\n\nAnother great aspect of Hookbots is how much variety it offers from match to match. A great example of this is in the campaign as you start off with a standard battle, then progress to 1 vs 20 matches, and then you even eventually end up playing play volleyball. These mixed modes are even further expanded upon in the party segment of the game as different special events (like big head mode), level environmental hazards, and game modes are randomly mashed together.\n\nWhile the multiplayer modes are the highlight and are likely a blast to play with a group of friends there is still a fair amount of single-player content though it is definitely a bit limited. I found unlocking new items by playing the campaign as different characters was a ton of fun for me, but the single-player challenges were a bit let down as there are only five of them and you don’t seem to unlock anymore. However, between battle bots in the multiplayer mode (though the a.i. does seem to just fall off the map quite often in easy and normal mode), beating the campaign several times, and completing the challenges I still clocked in six fulfilling hours with more unlockables rewards I have yet to obtain. \n\nOverall, Hookbots has a fantastic core gameplay mechanic that is easy to learn and offers more nuanced movement depth with continued play. The excellent damage system as robots visually fall apart and scramble to retrieve their lost body pieces before opponents destroy them is outstanding. The great battle controls combined with the large array of interesting multiplayer modes makes the title a really great party game. While the single-player experience is enjoyable, though a bit light on content. But with the unlockables and bot players, it still kept me playing for hours.",
